[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "2607252700"
  :adr/title "Capability WIT v2 binds a non-forgeable grant resource to each typed operation"
  :adr/status :accepted
  :adr/date "2026-07-25"
  :adr/body "# Capability WIT v2\n\n## Decision\n\n`aiueos:capability@0.3.0` is the normative ABI for new effectful Kotoba Components. Version 1 and the pre-grant `0.2.0` package remain readable only for explicit compatibility deployments and MUST NOT gain new effects.\n\nEvery imported provider interface owns a `grant` resource.  The resource has no guest constructor, representation, serializer, transfer operation, or destructor with authority semantics.  Only the native Component linker creates it after it has verified the admitted descriptor, issuer, subject, audience, expiry, revocation epoch, and host identity.\n\nEach operation takes `borrow<grant>`, never a guest-supplied descriptor. `capability.acquire` is the sole host-issued path to an `own<grant>`; it is not a guest constructor and the host binds the resulting handle to one admitted lease. Thus target, operation, byte/item quotas, deadline, and audit identity are bound once by the host and cannot be widened by a guest request.  The guest supplies only typed operation data.\n\nThe seven v2 interfaces are `identity.sign`, `identity.verify`, `hash.sha256`, `http.post`, `log.read`, `log.append`, and `clock.now`.  Their payload and result records are defined in `wit/aiueos-capability-v2/aiueos-capability.wit`; providers must not overload them with EDN, JSON, pointer/length pairs, or host handles.\n\n## Enforcement\n\nBefore every operation the native adapter MUST verify the resource's immutable descriptor, current lease epoch, expiry, use count, byte count, deadline, and audit sink availability.  It MUST append an audit receipt containing the grant identity and operation outcome before returning success.  A revoke or audit failure is a denial.  No WASI import is implied by this package.\n\n## Migration\n\nA compiler may emit v2 only when all requested effects have v2 lowering.  A runtime may execute v1 only behind an explicit compatibility profile, with the same admission and lease checks; it must never lower a v2 Component to v1 or an ambient host call.  Conformance requires compiler artifact generation, native linking, provider execution, quota/revocation/audit negative tests, and an end-to-end Component test."}]
